There's https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/5a4483eb1c0918542298d1864b7233f962952880/src/libraries/System.Net.Sockets/tests/FunctionalTests/SendFile.cs#L213 

Trying something like
```c#
using var client = new Socket(AddressFamily.InterNetwork, SocketType.Stream, ProtocolType.Tcp);
using var listener = new Socket(AddressFamily.InterNetwork, SocketType.Stream, ProtocolType.Tcp);
listener.BindToAnonymousPort(IPAddress.Loopback);
listener.Listen(1);

client.Connect(listener.LocalEndPoint);
using Socket server = listener.Accept();
server.ForceNonBlocking(force: true);

Assert.Throws<InvalidOperationException>(() => server.SendFile(null));
```
won't throw the exception (i.e. that test fails) on Windows and Linux.
After `ForceNonBlocking` both `_willBlock` and `_willBlockInternal` are `false`, so https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/16e6e9530687b34cc93a85d4043093f06f9b7eff/src/libraries/System.Net.Sockets/src/System/Net/Sockets/Socket.cs#L4045-L4051 won't throw.

The following program sends 1000 MiB and runs just fine.
With the debugger it can be verifyed that it blocks until the send is finished (will step over once the client has read the sent data). I.e. it's the same behavior as when `server.Blocking = true`.

<details>
  <summary>SendFile non-blocking 1000 MiB</summary>

```c#
using System;
using System.Diagnostics;
using System.IO;
using System.Net;
using System.Net.Sockets;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

using var client = new Socket(AddressFamily.InterNetwork, SocketType.Stream, ProtocolType.Tcp);
using var listener = new Socket(AddressFamily.InterNetwork, SocketType.Stream, ProtocolType.Tcp);
listener.BindToAnonymousPort(IPAddress.Loopback);
listener.Listen(1);

client.Connect(listener.LocalEndPoint);
using Socket server = listener.Accept();
server.Blocking = false;

Task clientTask = RunClientAsync();

string fileName = Path.GetTempFileName();
try
{
    const long FileLength = 1000 * 1024 * 1024;
    using (FileStream fs = File.Create(fileName))
    {
        fs.SetLength(FileLength);
    }

    Debug.Assert(!server.Blocking);

    server.SendFile(fileName);
    server.Close();
}
catch (SocketException ex)
{
    Console.WriteLine(ex.Message);
    Console.WriteLine(ex.SocketErrorCode);
}
finally
{
    File.Delete(fileName);
}

await clientTask;

Task RunClientAsync() => Task.Run(() =>
{
    byte[] buffer = new byte[4096];
    long totalReceived = 0;
    int received = 0;
    while ((received = client.Receive(buffer)) > 0)
    {
        Console.Write('.');
        totalReceived += received;
    }

    Console.WriteLine($"\nClient received {totalReceived / 1024 / 1024} MiB");
});

internal static class SocketTestExtensions
{
    // Binds to an IP address and OS-assigned port. Returns the chosen port.
    public static int BindToAnonymousPort(this Socket socket, IPAddress address)
    {
        socket.Bind(new IPEndPoint(address, 0));
        return ((IPEndPoint)socket.LocalEndPoint).Port;
    }
}
```
</details>

There is something strange. When in the above code 
```diff
server.Blocking = false;
+DumpState(server);
```
```c#
static void DumpState(Socket socket)
{
    BindingFlags flags = BindingFlags.Instance | BindingFlags.NonPublic;
    FieldInfo _willBlock = typeof(Socket).GetField("_willBlock", flags);
    FieldInfo _willBlockInternal = typeof(Socket).GetField("_willBlockInternal", flags);

    object willBlock = _willBlock.GetValue(socket);
    object willBlockInternal = _willBlockInternal.GetValue(socket);

    Console.WriteLine($"Blocking: {socket.Blocking}\nwillBlock: {willBlock}\nwillBlockInternal: {willBlockInternal}\n");
}
```
Then on Windows it's the same behaviour. It runs fine whether `server.Blocking` is true/false.

On Linux it gets flaky with this line. Means on some runs it fails immediately (expected), on some runs it finished without failure or it fails somewhere in the middle.
Seems there's a race-condition anywhere?
